## Part 3: The first slide

Let's start by getting a single photo to fill the entire screen. Update `+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<div class="gallery">
  <div class="slide">
    <img
      src="/photos/{data.photos[0].filename}"
      alt={data.photos[0].title}
    />
  </div>
</div>
```

Save and check your browser. You'll see the image, but it's sitting on the page at its natural size, surrounded by the template's default padding and margins.

We need to break out of the template's layout entirely. Add a style block at the bottom of the file:

```svelte
<style>
  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .gallery {
    height: 100dvh;
    overflow-y: scroll;
  }

  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    position: relative;
    background: black;
  }

  .slide img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
</style>
```

Several important things are happening here.

The [`:global(body)`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/global-styles) rule overrides the template's default body styles. The `:global()` wrapper tells Svelte to apply this style to the whole page rather than scoping it to this component only. We remove all margins and padding and set `overflow: hidden` so only our gallery scrolls, not the page itself.

The `.gallery` container takes up the full height of the viewport using [`100dvh`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/length#dynamic_viewport_units). The `dvh` unit stands for "dynamic viewport height." Unlike the older `vh` unit, it accounts for the browser toolbars that appear and disappear on mobile phones. When you scroll down on your phone and the address bar hides itself, `100dvh` expands to fill the newly available space. This is essential for a full-screen mobile experience.

Each `.slide` also fills exactly `100dvh`, so one slide equals one screen.

The `img` is set to fill its parent completely. But what about photos that aren't the same shape as the screen? That's where [`object-fit: cover`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/object-fit) comes in. It scales the image up until it fills the container, cropping whatever doesn't fit rather than squishing or stretching. The result is a full-bleed photo with no letterboxing or empty space.

Save and check your browser. The first photo should fill the entire screen, edge to edge.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/first-slide-fullscreen.png"
  alt="A single Gordon Parks photograph filling the entire browser viewport with no margins, padding, or template chrome visible"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

Try resizing your browser window. The image adapts smoothly to any shape — tall, wide, square. That's `object-fit: cover` at work.

## Part 4: All the slides, with scroll snap

Now let's show all the photos. We only want to render the photo-type slides for now. Replace the single slide with a filtered loop:

```svelte
<div class="gallery">
  {#each data.photos.filter(p => p.type === 'photo') as photo}
    <div class="slide">
      <img
        src="/photos/{photo.filename}"
        alt={photo.title}
      />
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Save and scroll. You'll see all the photo slides stacked, each filling the screen. But the scrolling is free — you can stop halfway between two photos, which looks bad.

This is where scroll snap comes in. Add two CSS properties:

```svelte
<style>
  /* ... existing styles ... */

  .gallery {
    height: 100dvh;
    overflow-y: scroll;
    scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
  }

  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    position: relative;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
</style>
```

Two new lines, and the whole experience transforms.

[`scroll-snap-type: y mandatory`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/scroll-snap-type) on the gallery tells the browser: "When scrolling stops on the vertical axis, snap to the nearest snap point. Always. No exceptions."

[`scroll-snap-align: start`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/scroll-snap-align) on each slide says: "My snap point is my top edge." So when snapping happens, the top of a slide aligns with the top of the viewport.

Save and scroll again. Each swipe or scroll click locks precisely to the next photo. On a phone, this will feel like swiping through a story.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/scroll-snap-working.png"
  alt="Two side-by-side screenshots showing the gallery mid-scroll and then snapped cleanly to a photo"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="600px"
/>

This is one of those moments where a very small amount of CSS creates a dramatic change. Two properties, zero JavaScript, and the page feels like a native app.

## Part 5: Caption overlays

The photos fill the screen but the reader has no context. Let's overlay captions on each image, the way Reels and Stories put text at the bottom of the frame.

Update the slide markup:

```svelte
<div class="gallery">
  {#each data.photos.filter(p => p.type === 'photo') as photo}
    <div class="slide">
      <img
        src="/photos/{photo.filename}"
        alt={photo.title}
      />
      <div class="caption">
        <h2>{photo.title}</h2>
        <p>{photo.caption}</p>
        <span class="credit">{photo.credit}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Add styles for the caption:

```svelte
<style>
  /* ... existing styles ... */

  .caption {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85) 0%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4) 70%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    color: white;
  }

  .caption h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .caption p {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  .caption .credit {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }
</style>
```

The key trick is [`position: absolute`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/position) on the caption combined with `position: relative` on the slide. This pulls the caption out of the normal document flow and pins it relative to the slide container. `bottom: 0` anchors it to the bottom edge.

The [`linear-gradient`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/gradient/linear-gradient) background creates a fade from near-black at the bottom to transparent at the top. This ensures the white text is readable regardless of what's in the photo behind it. It's the same technique used by Instagram, YouTube, and every streaming service that overlays text on images.

