## Part 6: Extracting a PhotoSlide component

Our markup and styles are getting long. Let's extract the slide into its own component to keep things clean — the same principle we practiced in [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/).

Create `src/lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { photo } = $props();
</script>

<div class="slide">
  <img
    src="/photos/{photo.filename}"
    alt={photo.title}
  />
  <div class="caption">
    <h2>{photo.title}</h2>
    <p>{photo.caption}</p>
    <span class="credit">{photo.credit}</span>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    position: relative;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }

  .slide img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .caption {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to top,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85) 0%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4) 70%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    color: white;
  }

  .caption h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .caption p {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  .caption .credit {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }
</style>
```

Now simplify `+page.svelte`. The page file controls the gallery container and the global overrides; each slide owns its own layout and styles.

```svelte
<script>
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<div class="gallery">
  {#each data.photos.filter(p => p.type === 'photo') as photo}
    <PhotoSlide {photo} />
  {/each}
</div>

<style>
  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .gallery {
    height: 100dvh;
    overflow-y: scroll;
    scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
  }
</style>
```

Save and verify it all still works.

## Part 7: A title slide

Right now the gallery opens straight into the first photo. For a journalism project we want to give the reader a headline and introduction before the photos begin — but styled as part of the experience, not as a traditional article header.

Create `src/lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { headline, intro, byline } = $props();
</script>

<div class="slide">
  <div class="content">
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="intro">{intro}</p>
    <p class="byline">{byline}</p>
    <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll to begin ↓</p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .content {
    max-width: 600px;
    padding: 2rem;
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .intro {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.85;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .byline {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    opacity: 0.6;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }

  .scroll-hint {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    opacity: 0.4;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
```

Import it and place it before the photo loop in `+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<div class="gallery">
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the federal government. His camera found dignity in the everyday — newsboys, neighbors, children, and the streets they shared."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos.filter(p => p.type === 'photo') as photo}
    <PhotoSlide {photo} />
  {/each}
</div>
```

Save and check. The gallery now opens on a black screen with the headline centered in the viewport. Scroll down to enter the photographs.

