## Part 8: Interstitial text slides

Right now the gallery moves straight through all the photos in sequence. But the story has chapters — Parks documented newsboys, then street scenes, then families, then community spaces. We can use the `"text"` entries in our data to add context cards between the photographs, breaking the sequence into sections the way a book uses chapter titles.

Create `src/lib/components/TextSlide.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { slide } = $props();
</script>

<div class="slide">
  <div class="content">
    <h2>{slide.headline}</h2>
    <p>{slide.body}</p>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .content {
    max-width: 560px;
    padding: 2rem;
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    opacity: 0.8;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
```

Now update `+page.svelte` to loop over all slides — not just photos — and use an `{#if}` block to render the right component for each type. This is the same conditional rendering pattern you learned in [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/#part-3-creating-a-parent-component), now applied to a real editorial problem.

```svelte
<script>
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';
  import TextSlide from '$lib/components/TextSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<div class="gallery">
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the federal government. His camera found dignity in the everyday — newsboys, neighbors, children, and the streets they shared."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos as slide}
    {#if slide.type === 'photo'}
      <PhotoSlide photo={slide} />
    {:else if slide.type === 'text'}
      <TextSlide {slide} />
    {/if}
  {/each}
</div>
```

Save and scroll through. The text cards appear between the photographs exactly where they sit in `photos.json`. You can reorder the story by editing the JSON file — no code changes needed.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/text-slide.png"
  alt="A black screen with centered white text showing a chapter heading in uppercase and a paragraph of editorial context beneath it"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

This is a meaningful editorial pattern. The `type` field in the data lets a reporter or editor control the narrative structure without touching any Svelte code. A non-technical collaborator could open `photos.json`, reorder slides, change captions, and reshape the story entirely.

## Part 9: A slide counter

As readers scroll through, it helps to know where they are. Let's add a small counter in the corner — something like "5 / 15" — that updates as they move through the gallery.

We'll use a browser feature called [`IntersectionObserver`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API) to detect which slide is currently on screen. Don't worry about memorizing every detail of the API — it's the kind of thing you look up when you need it. The important thing to understand is what it does: it watches a set of elements and fires a callback whenever they enter or leave the viewport.

### Introducing `onMount`

To use IntersectionObserver, we need a reference to the actual HTML elements in the page. But here's the catch: when a Svelte component's script block first runs, the HTML hasn't been created yet. The elements don't exist in the browser at that point.

That's what [`onMount`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/lifecycle-hooks#onMount) is for. It's a Svelte function that runs your code *after* the page has loaded and all the HTML elements are present in the browser. Anything that needs to interact with real DOM elements — like setting up an observer, initializing a map, or measuring element sizes — belongs in `onMount`.

Update the script block in `+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { onMount } from 'svelte';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';
  import TextSlide from '$lib/components/TextSlide.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  let currentIndex = $state(0);
  let galleryEl;

  onMount(() => {
    const slides = galleryEl.querySelectorAll('[data-index]');

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            currentIndex = parseInt(entry.target.dataset.index);
          }
        });
      },
      {
        root: galleryEl,
        threshold: 0.5,
      }
    );

    slides.forEach((slide) => observer.observe(slide));

    return () => observer.disconnect();
  });

  let photoCount = $derived(data.photos.filter(p => p.type === 'photo').length);
  let photoIndex = $derived(
    data.photos
      .slice(0, currentIndex)
      .filter(p => p.type === 'photo')
      .length
  );
</script>
```

Let's walk through this carefully.

`currentIndex` is a `$state` variable tracking which slide is currently visible, counting from zero across all slide types.

`galleryEl` will hold a reference to the gallery container HTML element — we'll wire that up in the markup using `bind:this`.

Inside `onMount`, we find all elements with a `data-index` attribute (we'll add those next), then create an `IntersectionObserver` that fires whenever a slide is at least 50% visible (`threshold: 0.5`). When that happens, we update `currentIndex` to match.

The `return () => observer.disconnect()` at the end is cleanup code. When Svelte destroys the component, it calls this function to stop observing, which prevents memory leaks.

The two `$derived` variables at the bottom calculate what to show in the counter. `photoCount` is the total number of photo slides. `photoIndex` counts how many photo slides appear before the current position in the sequence — so text and title slides don't inflate the count.

Now update the markup to wire everything together. Note the three changes: `bind:this` on the gallery, `data-index` on each slide wrapper, and the counter element outside the gallery:

```svelte
<div class="gallery" bind:this={galleryEl}>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the federal government. His camera found dignity in the everyday — newsboys, neighbors, children, and the streets they shared."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each data.photos as slide, i}
    <div data-index={i + 1}>
      {#if slide.type === 'photo'}
        <PhotoSlide photo={slide} />
      {:else if slide.type === 'text'}
        <TextSlide {slide} />
      {/if}
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>

{#if photoIndex > 0}
  <div class="counter">
    {photoIndex} / {photoCount}
  </div>
{/if}
```

[`bind:this={galleryEl}`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/bind#bind:this) gives us a live reference to the gallery's HTML element, which we pass to IntersectionObserver as its scroll container.

The `{#each}` now includes `, i` after `slide` to get the index of each item in the loop. Each slide is wrapped in a `<div data-index={i + 1}>` so the observer knows which position it's watching.

The counter only appears when `photoIndex > 0` — it stays hidden on the title slide and the first text card, appearing only once the reader has scrolled into the photos.

Add styles for the counter:

```svelte
<style>
  /* ... existing styles ... */

  .counter {
    position: fixed;
    top: 1rem;
    right: 1rem;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
    color: white;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    z-index: 10;
  }
</style>
```

The counter is `position: fixed` so it stays in the top-right corner as you scroll. [`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/font-variant-numeric) makes digits fixed-width so the counter doesn't shift when the number changes.

Save and scroll through. The counter appears after the title slide and updates with each snap.

