## Part 10: A credits slide

Just as we opened with a title, let's close with a credits slide that tells the reader more about where these photographs came from. This is the equivalent of the `MethodologyBox` we used in Week 9, but presented as part of the visual experience rather than appended below it.

Create `src/lib/components/CreditsSlide.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { children } = $props();
</script>

<div class="slide">
  <div class="content">
    {@render children()}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .content {
    max-width: 600px;
    padding: 2rem;
    color: white;
  }

  .content :global(h2) {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .content :global(p) {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.85;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .content :global(a) {
    color: white;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .content :global(a:hover) {
    opacity: 1;
  }
</style>
```

This uses the `children` prop pattern from [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/) so we can write free-form HTML inside the component tags. The `:global()` selectors inside the scoped styles reach into the passed-in children to style them.

Import it in `+page.svelte` and add it after the `{#each}` loop, before the closing `</div>`:

```svelte
import CreditsSlide from '$lib/components/CreditsSlide.svelte';
```

```svelte
  <CreditsSlide>
    <h2>About These Photographs</h2>
    <p>
      Gordon Parks (1912–2006) was a self-taught photographer, filmmaker,
      writer, and composer. In 1942, he became the first Black photographer
      hired by the Farm Security Administration. His Harlem photographs are
      held by the Library of Congress and are in the public domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      High-resolution scans of the original negatives are available for
      free download from the
      <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/search/?q=gordon+parks+harlem" target="_blank">
        Library of Congress Prints &amp; Photographs Division</a>.
    </p>
  </CreditsSlide>
```

Save and scroll to the end. After the last photograph, you'll land on a clean black screen with the story behind the images.

