## Part 11: Testing on your phone

We've been building on a desktop browser, but the whole point of this design is mobile. Let's see how it actually feels on a phone.

SvelteKit's development server is already running on your computer. Your phone can access it if both devices are on the same WiFi network.

Find your computer's local IP address. On Mac, open System Settings, click Network, and look for your IP — it will be something like `192.168.1.42`. On Windows, open a command prompt and type `ipconfig`.

On your phone's browser, navigate to `http://192.168.1.42:5173`, replacing the IP with your own.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/phone-preview.png"
  alt="A hand holding a smartphone displaying the Gordon Parks photo gallery with a full-screen portrait photograph and caption overlay at the bottom"
  chromeTitle="Phone Preview"
  chromeUrl="http://192.168.1.42:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

Swipe up through the gallery. Each slide should snap into place. The captions should be readable against the gradient. The counter should tick upward.

This is the experience we designed for. On a phone it doesn't feel like a website. It feels like a story.

If anything looks off — text getting cut off, the address bar causing layout jumps — this is where the `dvh` unit pays off. It adapts to the dynamic viewport height as the browser chrome appears and disappears.

Take a few minutes to swipe through on your phone and note what works and what you'd change. We'll discuss as a class.

## Part 12: Polish

A few small refinements that add up to a more professional experience.

### Smoother scrolling on iOS

```css
.gallery {
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
}
```

This enables momentum scrolling on iOS, making swipes feel more fluid.

### Hiding the scrollbar

On desktop a scrollbar is visible on the right edge of the gallery. We can hide it while keeping scroll functionality:

```css
.gallery {
  scrollbar-width: none;
}

.gallery::-webkit-scrollbar {
  display: none;
}
```

`scrollbar-width: none` works in Firefox. `::-webkit-scrollbar` handles Chrome and Safari.

### Preventing overscroll bounce

```css
.gallery {
  overscroll-behavior: none;
}
```

This prevents the rubbery bounce effect at the top and bottom of the gallery on mobile.

### The complete gallery styles

With all the polish applied, the final `.gallery` styles in `+page.svelte` look like this:

```svelte
<style>
  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .gallery {
    height: 100dvh;
    overflow-y: scroll;
    scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    overscroll-behavior: none;
  }

  .gallery::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  .counter {
    position: fixed;
    top: 1rem;
    right: 1rem;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
    color: white;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    z-index: 10;
  }
</style>
```

Save, commit, and push to GitHub. Once the deploy succeeds, open your live site on your phone and swipe through the complete story.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own photo story

Find a collection of public domain photographs from a digital archive and build your own full-screen, mobile-first photo gallery. Good sources include:

* [Library of Congress](https://www.loc.gov/free-to-use/) — Free to Use and Reuse sets
* [NYPL Digital Collections](https://digitalcollections.nypl.org/) — filter for public domain
* [Smithsonian Open Access](https://www.si.edu/openaccess) — millions of images, all CC0
* [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/) — search by topic and license
* [National Archives](https://www.archives.gov/research/catalog) — US government photographs

Your project must have:

* A title slide with a headline and introduction
* At least 8 photographs in a scroll-snap gallery with caption overlays
* At least two interstitial text slides that provide editorial context
* A credits slide with source information and proper attribution
* A design that works on mobile — test it on your phone before submitting

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your photo story with the class. You should be prepared to:

* Show the story on your phone or in a narrow browser window
* Explain your editorial choices — which photos you selected, how you sequenced them, and what story you're telling
* Walk through one technical detail of your implementation
* Discuss how you found and verified the public domain status of your images
